Caesar shift that wraps and keeps case:
```
def shift_char(ch, n):
    if ch.islower():
        return chr((ord(ch) - ord('a') + n) % 26 + ord('a'))
    if ch.isupper():
        return chr((ord(ch) - ord('A') + n) % 26 + ord('A'))
    return ch

def whisper_cipher(text, shift):
    return "".join(shift_char(c, shift) for c in text)
```

Subtracting ord('a') maps the letter to 0-25, you shift there, `% 26`
wraps it, then you add ord('a') back. Python's `%` returns a non-negative
result for a positive modulus, so negative shifts work with no extra code.

Repeated divmod peels digits off the right:
```
DIGITS = "0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ"

def to_base(value, base):
    if value == 0:
        return "0"
    out = ""
    while value:
        value, rem = divmod(value, base)
        out = DIGITS[rem] + out      # prepend: digits come out backwards
    return out

def from_base(text, base):
    value = 0
    for ch in text.upper():
        value = value * base + DIGITS.index(ch)
    return value
```

Run-length encoding is the same "count a run, emit it" shape:
```
def compress(s):
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(s):
        j = i
        while j < len(s) and s[j] == s[i]:
            j += 1
        n = j - i
        out.append(s[i] if n == 1 else f"{s[i]}{n}")
        i = j
    return "".join(out)
```
